--- Finding (mutex): a function that only ever answers an error, handed to `revise`
--- as the revision's function, does not build compiled.
---
--- crow run -i:  no   (the revision fails, its reason is printed)
--- crow run:     error[E0308]: mismatched types --> src/main.rs:64:246
---               (the function is typed `-> CrowResult<()>`; revise's closure
---               wants `CrowResult<C>`, the record's type)
---               error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler
---               refused what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- Expected: no, in both modes.
---
--- The class of the known findings/json_pointer-1.co (an only-error value handed
--- to encode or str) and findings/monads_maybe_monad-1.co (one in a list
--- literal), at another site: a function that only fails takes no payload type
--- from the place that uses its answer. Called directly (`r = refused[C[0], 'no']`)
--- it builds. In the solution the failing revision is a withdrawal that fails
--- only when there is too little to take, so it answers a record on its other path.
use memory

struct C [n]

fn refused[c, why]
    return error[why, 'demo']

r = revise['x', C[0], refused, 'no']
log[r.reason]
